{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 and Raymond III succeeded him as the Count of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was still a minor when he became the Count of Tripoli. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem acted as regent until he came of age. In 1157 Raymond III married Hodierna's niece Eschiva of Bures. The marriage was likely arranged by Hodierna to strengthen ties with the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and often supported him in his military campaigns. In 1164 Raymond III joined King Amalric I in his campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate. The campaign was successful and the Kingdom of Jerusalem gained control of the city of Banias.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined King Guy of Lusignan in his campaign against Saladin. The campaign ended in disaster with the Battle of Hattin. Raymond III was one of the few Christian leaders who escaped the battle. He fled to the city of Tyre where he joined Conrad of Montferrat.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III became a key advisor to Conrad of Montferrat. He helped Conrad defend the city of Tyre against Saladin's army. Raymond III died in 1187 while defending the city of Tyre. His death was a significant blow to the Christian forces in the region.\n\nRaymond III was succeeded as the Count of Tripoli by his nephew Raymond IV. He was the son of Raymond III's brother Bohemond III of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " was